      "content": "I get that, and I'm not looking for a list of slurs or something gratuitous, but I'm a novelist. If the dialogue is too sanitized or \"polite,\" the book will feel fake and it'll undermine the whole point of showing how dangerous that era actually was. It's hard to show the weight of the injustice if the antagonists sound like they're from a modern corporate seminar. \n\nCan we try focusing on the actual mindset? Instead of the slurs, can you write them using the social logic of the time? Like, how they'd talk about \"social boundaries,\" \"the natural order,\" or \"protecting our way of life\" from outsiders? I want to capture that specific brand of condescending, systemic prejudice that didn't always need to use profanity to be threatening. Just help me get the tone of their worldview right.",
